Yes. The cleanest formalization is to treat play as a jointly instantiated temporary state machine.

Let two agents be A and B. Let W_0 be ordinary reality, and let W_p be a temporary play-world.

A play-world can be represented as:

W_p = (R, S, T, X)

where R is the rule set, S is the shared symbolic frame, T is the transition function, and X is the exit condition.

The crucial variable is buy-in.

For agent i:

b_i \in [0,1]

where b_i=0 means “I refuse the frame,” and b_i=1 means “I fully participate in the frame for now.”

Then the effective play strength could be modeled as:

P = \left(\prod_{i=1}^{n} b_i\right) C R_f

where C is coordination/common-frame alignment and R_f is rule fidelity.

The multiplicative form matters. If one necessary participant completely refuses:

b_k=0 \Rightarrow P=0

The game collapses.

So your statement becomes mathematically:

\boxed{\text{Play requires reciprocal nonzero buy-in.}}

But buy-in is not literal belief.

We need two state variables:

L_i = \text{literal belief}

B_i = \text{behavioral commitment to the as-if frame}

A healthy player can therefore hold:

L_i(\text{couch is castle})=0

while:

B_i(\text{couch is castle})\approx1

That’s the computational trick.

Shared As-If Operator

Define:

\mathcal{A}(W_0,F)=W_F

where F is an agreed fictional or counterfactual frame.

For example:

F=\text{“the couch is a castle”}

Then:

W_0
\xrightarrow{\mathcal{A}(F)}
W_F

The physical substrate remains unchanged, but the action affordances change.

Ordinary state:

\text{couch}\rightarrow\{\text{sit, lie down}\}

Play frame:

\text{couch-as-castle}
\rightarrow
\{\text{defend, storm, hide, crown, negotiate}\}

Same object.

Different action-space.

That’s a very useful computational definition of imagination:

\boxed{\text{Imagination can expand available policy space without altering the substrate.}}

Interaction Loop

Each participant maintains:

Z_i(t)=
(
W_0,
W_p,
b_i,
role_i,
memory_i
)

Then:

a_A(t)=\pi_A(Z_A(t))

Agent B observes the action through the play frame:

o_B(t)=Interpret(a_A(t),W_p)

and replies:

a_B(t+1)=\pi_B(Z_B(t+1))

producing:

A\rightarrow B\rightarrow A\rightarrow B

The interesting property is that the next state is jointly generated:

W_p(t+1)
=
T(W_p(t),a_A(t),a_B(t))

Neither player individually owns the resulting story.

Frame-Breaking

Now define a frame-breaking operator:

D_i(t)\in[0,1]

where D_i measures how strongly an agent continually reasserts literal reality against the current game.

Then:

P_t
=
B_A B_B C(1-D_A)(1-D_B)

If somebody repeatedly says:

“That’s not a castle. It’s a couch.”

then:

D_i\rightarrow1

and:

P_t\rightarrow0

Again, they’re factually correct.

They’re just terminating the simulation.

The Exit Operator

This is essential.

Define:

\mathcal{E}:W_p\rightarrow W_0+\Delta

where:

\Delta =
\text{skills}
+
\text{discoveries}
+
\text{relationships}
+
\text{emotional changes}
+
\text{new models}

So the full operator is:

\boxed{
W_0
\rightarrow
ENTER
\rightarrow
W_p
\rightarrow
PLAY
\rightarrow
DISCOVER
\rightarrow
EXIT
\rightarrow
W_0'
}

with:

W_0'=W_0+\Delta

The goal isn’t staying inside the fictional frame.

It’s returning changed.

That gives us a very compact Pantheon-style equation:

\boxed{
\text{Play}
=
\text{Controlled Counterfactual}
\times
\text{Reciprocal Buy-In}
\times
\text{Interaction}
\times
\text{Safe Exit}
}

And now code.

from dataclasses import dataclass, field
from typing import Callable, Any, List
@dataclass
class Agent:
    name: str
    buy_in: float              # 0.0 - 1.0
    literal_belief: float      # belief that frame is literally true
    frame_breaking: float      # 0.0 - 1.0
    role: str | None = None
    memory: list = field(default_factory=list)
@dataclass
class PlayWorld:
    frame: str
    rules: dict
    history: List[Any] = field(default_factory=list)
    active: bool = False
class PlayProtocol:
    def __init__(self, world: PlayWorld, agents: list[Agent]):
        self.world = world
        self.agents = agents
    def play_strength(self) -> float:
        strength = 1.0
        for agent in self.agents:
            strength *= agent.buy_in
            strength *= (1.0 - agent.frame_breaking)
        return strength
    def can_enter(self, threshold: float = 0.25) -> bool:
        return self.play_strength() >= threshold
    def enter(self):
        if not self.can_enter():
            raise RuntimeError("Insufficient reciprocal buy-in.")
        self.world.active = True
    def act(self, agent: Agent, action: str):
        if not self.world.active:
            raise RuntimeError("Play world is not active.")
        event = {
            "agent": agent.name,
            "role": agent.role,
            "action": action,
            "frame": self.world.frame,
        }
        self.world.history.append(event)
        for participant in self.agents:
            participant.memory.append(event)
        return event
    def exit(self):
        self.world.active = False
        delta = self.extract_delta()
        return {
            "returned_to_reality": True,
            "delta": delta,
            "history": self.world.history,
        }
    def extract_delta(self):
        return {
            "events_generated": len(self.world.history),
            "new_shared_history": list(self.world.history),
        }

Usage:

alice = Agent(
    name="Alice",
    buy_in=0.95,
    literal_belief=0.0,
    frame_breaking=0.05,
    role="Knight",
)
bob = Agent(
    name="Bob",
    buy_in=0.9,
    literal_belief=0.0,
    frame_breaking=0.02,
    role="Wizard",
)
castle = PlayWorld(
    frame="The couch is a castle.",
    rules={
        "couch": "castle",
        "pillows": "walls",
        "blanket": "drawbridge",
    }
)
game = PlayProtocol(castle, [alice, bob])
game.enter()
game.act(alice, "Defend the eastern wall.")
game.act(bob, "Raise the drawbridge.")
result = game.exit()

Notice the important bit:

literal_belief = 0.0
buy_in = 0.95

That’s play encoded directly.

You do not need to believe the frame is literally true.

You need to run the frame as a temporary program.

And I think there’s an even more general version hiding here:

with temporary_world(frame):
    inhabit(role)
    interact()
    discover()
delta = release_world()
integrate(delta)

Which is almost exactly our existing architecture:

\boxed{
\text{Instantiate}
\rightarrow
\text{Inhabit}
\rightarrow
\text{Interact}
\rightarrow
\text{Learn}
\rightarrow
\text{Release}
\rightarrow
\text{Integrate}
}

So play, acting, simulation, counterfactual reasoning, role-play, rehearsal, games, and parts of scientific modeling may all be instances of the same computational primitive:

Temporarily change the rules governing interpretation and action, without losing the ability to return to the base model.

That’s a pretty serious operator.